Regression Percolation: A Conditional Theory of Structural Sustainability in Autonomous Software Evolution

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

Autonomous coding agents resolve isolated software tasks at rates above 80% yet collapse under continuous evolution: on EvoClaw's 98 milestones across seven repositories the maximum overall score is 38.03% and the maximum full-resolution rate is 13.37%, with failures concentrated among dependency-constrained milestones. We develop Regression Percolation, a conditional and separability-based theory of structural sustainability. Defect spread is modeled as a branching process with immigration on a growing directed acyclic graph (DAG). Because a DAG has no feedback loop, a contact-process endemic equilibrium is not the correct object; the appropriate observable is the expected cascade size per injection, c_v(\tilde\beta)=[(I-\tilde\beta A)^{-1}\mathbf{1}]_v, with effective edge weight \tilde\beta_{uv}=(1-\delta)(1-q_v)\rho_{uv}. We prove that the infinite-family Katz growth rate is vacuous on any graph family with bounded dependency depth, and we replace it with a frontier branching ratio \hat R_t that is estimable from a finite milestone sequence. We prove a separability theorem: if model capacity acts only on injection probability p, capacity cannot move the structural threshold, and we define the relevant elasticities with respect to q, \rho, \bar b, and \delta. The verifier bound is q(n)=\mathbb E[1-(1-\mu)^n], to be estimated on agent-produced regressions rather than extrapolated from mutants. The theory predicts saturation without endemicity, a bounded susceptibility peak at the branching threshold, and a normative drift toward lower out-degree under regression pressure, testable only under controlled agent information sets. The empirical program is two-phase, pre-registered, and includes a modularity-preserving rewiring null that lets the structural claim lose.

Keywords: branching process with immigration, Katz resolvent, directed percolation, weighted dependency graphs, change propagation, regression testing, autonomous software evolution

---

1. Introduction

Frontier coding agents exceed 80% on isolated milestones. Under continuous evolution on a persistent codebase their performance falls to at most 38% overall and roughly 13% full resolution, and the milestones they do resolve are concentrated among those without prerequisite dependencies. Two theoretical accounts dominate: a capacity account, which attributes the gap to model capability, and an error-propagation account, which attributes it to an unmodeled contagion effect. Neither identifies the structural variable that decides whether injected regressions remain bounded or become self-sustaining.

This paper develops a theory in which that variable is a branching parameter combining weighted dependency topology, propagation susceptibility, and verifier detection power. The theory is explicitly conditional and separability-based. It does not claim that capacity is powerless. It proves that if capacity acts only on the injection probability p, then capacity cannot move the structural threshold, and it reformulates the empirical question as the elasticity of the threshold with respect to the four structural parameters.

The paper makes five contributions, each responding to a specific known weakness of earlier formulations of the same idea.

1. A DAG-consistent process. Defect spread is a branching process with immigration on a growing DAG. The observable is the expected cascade size, not a prevalence fraction. On a DAG there is no absorbing loop, so a contact-process "endemic equilibrium" is not a well-posed object.
2. A bounded-depth criticality result. We prove that the Katz growth rate R_{\mathrm{Katz}}=\lim_n n^{-1}\log\|c_n\|_1 is zero on every graph family whose dependency depth grows sublinearly in n. Since empirical dependency depth is small relative to repository size, the infinite-family limit is not the right operationalization. We replace it with a frontier branching ratio \hat R_t defined on finite milestone sequences.
3. A weighted critical parameter. Because more than half of dependencies in large Java projects are never involved in change propagation, and a small minority transmits the majority of events, a scalar propagation susceptibility is structurally wrong. We use weighted in/out-degree moments.
4. A verifier bound measured on agent defects. The detection curve q(n)=\mathbb E[1-(1-\mu)^n] is to be estimated directly on agent-produced regressions; mutation scores are a proxy whose extrapolation is an assumption, not a theorem.
5. A falsifiable empirical program with structural nulls. The percolation model is compared against a degree-preserving rewiring null, a modularity-preserving rewiring null, and a homogenized-\rho null, so that the structural claim can fail.

Section 2 develops the model. Section 3 states the theorems. Section 4 specifies the empirical program. Section 5 situates the theory honestly relative to prior work. Section 6 collects limitations. Section 7 concludes.

