2. Model

2.1 Growing weighted dependency DAG

Let G_t=(V_t,E_t) be the dependency DAG at milestone index t. A directed edge u\to v means that v depends on u. Write \bar b_t=|E_t|/|V_t| for mean out-degree and L_t for the length of the longest directed path. An active regression at u contaminates out-neighbor v with edge-specific probability \rho_{uv}. Heterogeneity is not a refinement: in 35 large Java projects, more than half of all dependencies were never involved in change propagation, and a small percentage of dependencies transmitted the majority of propagation events. A scalar \rho would misrepresent that concentration. We therefore treat \rho_{uv} as the primitive object and define the weighted out-degree and weighted in-degree

k^{w}_{\mathrm{out}}(v)=\sum_{u:\,v\to u}\rho_{vu},
\qquad
k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}(v)=\sum_{u:\,u\to v}\rho_{uv}.

2.2 Branching process with immigration

At each newly added milestone v, the agent injects an active regression with probability

p'(v)=p_v\bigl(1-q_v\bigr),

where p_v is the injection probability and q_v is the detection probability at v. An active regression at u contaminates v with probability \rho_{uv}; the contamination escapes verification with probability (1-q_v)\rho_{uv}. Active sites are repaired at rate \delta; write \gamma=1-\delta for the per-step survival probability of an active site. Define the effective edge weight

\tilde\beta_{uv}=\gamma\,(1-q_v)\,\rho_{uv}.

Because the graph is acyclic, the process has no feedback loop: an active defect at u can contaminate descendants but cannot return to u. The correct object is therefore a branching process with immigration, not an undirected contact process. The immigration rate at milestone t is \iota_t=p'\bar b_t, and the central observable is the expected cascade size from an injection at v,

c_v(\tilde\beta)=\Bigl[(I-\tilde\beta A)^{-1}\mathbf{1}\Bigr]_v
=\sum_{k=0}^{L_t}\bigl(\tilde\beta A\bigr)^k\mathbf{1}\Big|_{v},

where A is the adjacency matrix of G_t and the sum is finite because A is nilpotent with index L_t+1. The component c_v is the expected total number of sites ever activated, including v itself, by a defect injected at v.

2.3 Bounded-depth degeneracy of the Katz growth rate

The Katz growth rate is defined along a graph family \{G_n\} by

R_{\mathrm{Katz}}(\tilde\beta)=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\log\|c_n(\tilde\beta)\|_1 .

Proposition 1 (bounded-depth degeneracy). Let G_n have longest path L_n and maximum out-degree d_n. Then for every \tilde\beta>0,

\|c_n(\tilde\beta)\|_\infty\le
\sum_{k=0}^{L_n}\bigl(\tilde\beta d_n\bigr)^k
\le (L_n+1)\max\bigl\{1,(\tilde\beta d_n)^{L_n}\bigr\}.

Consequently, if L_n=o(n) and d_n is bounded, then R_{\mathrm{Katz}}(\tilde\beta)=0 for every \tilde\beta>0. A nonzero growth rate requires dependency depth growing linearly in n.

Proof. The first inequality follows from \|A^k\mathbf{1}\|_\infty\le d_n^k and the nilpotency of A above index L_n; the second is elementary. Taking logarithms, dividing by n, and using L_n=o(n) gives the claim. ∎

The empirical implication is direct. Dependency depth in real software is small relative to repository size, so the infinite-family Katz limit is not the correct criticality object. The limit does not describe a phase transition that software systems actually undergo. This is a demotion, and it is stated as such.

2.4 Frontier branching ratio

We replace the infinite-family limit with a finite-sequence object. Let X_t\subseteq V_t be the set of active sites at milestone t and let \mathcal F_t\subseteq X_t be the active frontier, i.e. active sites with at least one out-neighbor in V_t\setminus\bigcup_{s\le t}X_s. Define the frontier branching ratio

\hat R_t
=
\gamma\,
\frac{1}{|\mathcal F_t|}
\sum_{v\in\mathcal F_t}\ \sum_{u:\,v\to u}\rho_{vu},

the expected number of newly activated sites per active frontier site per generation. In the homogeneous mean-field case this reduces to \hat R=\gamma(1-q)\rho\,\bar b, and in the correlated case its population analogue is

R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}
=
\gamma(1-q)\,
\frac{\langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}k^{w}_{\mathrm{out}}\rangle}
{\langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}\rangle},

which is the weighted directed analogue of the branching reproduction number. The angle brackets denote the joint weighted in/out moment. When in- and out-degrees are uncorrelated and weights are homogeneous, R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}} reduces to the familiar expression.

The estimator \hat R_t is a plug-in statistic: \rho_{uv} is estimated from human co-change histories in Phase 1, and \mathcal F_t is observed on the agent benchmark in Phase 2. Its sampling distribution is governed by the number of active frontier sites and by the variance of \rho_{uv} across observed edges. Consistency in the number of observed propagations follows from the law of large numbers under independent edge realizations; the finite-sample bias is O(|\mathcal F_t|^{-1/2}) under bounded \rho_{uv}.

2.5 Verifier detection curve

Let a defect have failure probability \mu on a randomly drawn test, and let the verifier run n tests. Then

q(n)=\mathbb E\bigl[1-(1-\mu)^n\bigr].

If the defect-severity distribution has a heavy tail near \mu=0, as mutation studies suggest, then q(n) is bounded away from 1 at every finite n; if it has bounded support away from zero, q(n)\to 1 exponentially. The curve is to be estimated on agent-produced regressions, not extrapolated from mutants. Mutation scores measure the ability of a suite to kill syntactic flips; agent regressions are semantic omissions of invariants. The two distributions of \mu need not coincide. The mutation-based estimate is an assumption whose validity must be checked against the direct estimate.

