3. Results

3.1 Separability theorem

Theorem 1 (separability). Suppose model capacity C acts on the injection probability p but not on q, \rho, \bar b, or \delta. Then for every p>0 the branching parameter R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}} is independent of p. Consequently, if R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}>1 and dependency depth is unbounded, the expected cascade size is unbounded for every p>0, and no increase in C below the threshold-crossing value of p changes the regime.

Proof. The immigration rate \iota=p'\bar b enters the expected cascade size additively and does not enter the branching parameter. The threshold is the zero of the branching operator, which is independent of p. ∎

The theorem is conditional. The antecedent—that capacity acts only on p—is an empirical hypothesis, not a theorem. A better model may write more modular code (lower \bar b), generate more effective tests (higher q), or produce patches that are easier to repair (higher \delta). The correct empirical target is the elasticity vector

\varepsilon_x=\frac{x}{R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}}\frac{\partial R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}}{\partial x},
\qquad x\in\{q,\rho,\bar b,\delta\},

together with \varepsilon_p=0 under the antecedent. Under the mean-field form,

\varepsilon_q=-\frac{q}{1-q},
\qquad
\varepsilon_\rho=1,
\qquad
\varepsilon_\delta=-\frac{\delta}{1-\delta},

and \varepsilon_{\bar b} is determined by the weighted degree moment. The separability hypothesis predicts |\varepsilon_q|,|\varepsilon_\rho|,|\varepsilon_{\bar b}|,|\varepsilon_\delta|\gg|\varepsilon_p|=0. This is a testable asymmetry, not a definitional truth.

3.2 Saturation without endemicity

In the supercritical regime the expected cascade size from a random injection is large but finite for every finite L_t. In quasi-steady state with immigration rate \iota_t, the active fraction satisfies

\frac{\mathbb E|X_t|}{|V_t|}
\approx
\iota_t\sum_{k=0}^{L_t}\hat R_t^{\,k}
=
\iota_t\frac{1-\hat R_t^{\,L_t+1}}{1-\hat R_t}
\quad(\hat R_t\neq 1),

with the value \iota_t(L_t+1) at \hat R_t=1. Three consequences follow. First, the active fraction saturates at a level determined by the cascade-size distribution and the immigration rate. Second, saturation is bounded by \iota_t(L_t+1) even in the supercritical regime, because depth is finite. Third, the phrase "endemic floor" is misleading: there is no nontrivial stationary density of an undirected contact process; there is saturation of a branching process with immigration on a finite-depth DAG. The observed regression rate in EvoClaw is consistent with saturation of this form; it is not evidence of an endemic equilibrium.

In the subcritical regime \hat R_t<1 and the cascade size decays geometrically in L_t with ratio \hat R_t. The correct statement is therefore saturation without endemicity: the infected fraction is bounded because the graph is finite and shallow, not because a nontrivial equilibrium exists.

3.3 Bounded susceptibility peak

Define the susceptibility \chi_p=\partial\,\mathbb E[c]/\partial p. On a graph of depth L and mean new out-degree \bar b_{\mathrm{new}},

\chi_p
=
\bar b_{\mathrm{new}}\,
\frac{1-\hat R^{\,L+1}}{1-\hat R}
\qquad(\hat R\neq 1),

which is maximized at \hat R=1 with value \bar b_{\mathrm{new}}(L+1) and decays to \bar b_{\mathrm{new}} as \hat R\to 0. The prediction is a peak of finite height near the branching threshold, not a divergence. The finiteness of the peak is a direct consequence of bounded depth and is the honest analogue of the divergence obtained in infinite directed percolation. Estimating the peak requires experimental variation in p, not observational variation in model identity, because model identity is confounded with q, \rho, and \bar b.

3.4 Closed-loop normative benchmark and the criticality prediction

Suppose the agent chooses out-degree d_v subject to a cost c(d_v) and a regression penalty proportional to the expected cascade size, and solves

\min_{d_v\ge 0}\ c(d_v)+\kappa\,\mathbb E[c](d_v)
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
c'(d_v)+\kappa\frac{\partial \mathbb E[c]}{\partial d_v}=0 .

If \partial \mathbb E[c]/\partial d_v>0, the optimal degree is strictly below the cost-minimizing degree. This is a normative benchmark: it specifies what an agent with knowledge of the structural parameters would do. It is not a behavioral prediction about current agents, which operate under partial observability, context-window constraints, and framework heuristics.

If the agent additionally observes regression pressure and adjusts \bar b in response, \bar b_{t+1}=\bar b_t-\eta\,\partial\mathbb E[c]/\partial\bar b, then the closed-loop system drifts toward the vicinity of \hat R_t\approx 1. This is a prediction of the closed-loop model. It is not a consequence of Lehman's laws, which characterize E-type evolution as a multi-level, multi-loop, multi-agent feedback system but do not supply a mathematical form or a criticality claim. The correct attribution is: Lehman motivates the closing of the loop; the criticality prediction is ours and is falsifiable by giving the agent a Katz-resolvent diagnostic versus not, and measuring whether \bar b_t stabilizes near the threshold.

