4. Empirical Program

4.1 Phase 1: calibration on human change-propagation histories

Before agent evaluation, structural parameters must be estimated on human-authored code, where the dependency graph and the propagation record are independent of the agent.

(i) Propagation susceptibility \rho_{uv}. For each change to file u, identify downstream dependents v modified in the same episode but not directly edited. The empirical frequency estimates \rho_{uv}, stratified by dependency type. Report the zero-inflation of \rho_{uv}, because Geipel and Schweitzer show that more than half of dependencies are never involved in change propagation. Report the depth distribution L_t because Proposition 1 makes it the operative quantity. Note the authors' own caution that dependency structure alone is a poor measure of change dynamics; the stratified estimate is a partial correction, not a refutation.

(ii) Visibility-matrix propagation cost. Compute the MacCormack–Rusnak–Baldwin propagation cost. Mozilla's propagation cost fell from 17.4% to 2.7% after a modularity-oriented rewrite, while Linux's was 7.4%. This is a structural covariate for the frontier branching ratio, not a substitute for it.

(iii) Detection q(n). Estimate the detection curve on agent-produced regressions from the EvoClaw runs, at test budgets n, and separately on mutation faults. Report both curves. If the agent-defect curve has a heavy tail near \mu=0, the hypothesis that no finite budget achieves q\to 1 is supported. This is a feasibility check: 98 milestones may not yield enough regression instances to estimate a heavy tail, in which case the direct curve must wait for Phase 2 expansion.

(iv) Repair rate \delta. Estimate time-to-repair distributions from version histories. Treat \delta as a sensitivity parameter until agent-specific repair data exist. Human repair-time distributions provide a lower bound at best; agent regressions may differ in persistence because of context loss and incomplete patches.

(v) Condensation structure. Compute strongly connected components of the file-level dependency graph and form the condensation DAG via Tarjan's algorithm. Report the fraction of edges intra- versus inter-SCC. The Hashimoto matrix is nilpotent on a DAG; the non-backtracking criterion applies only to cyclic subgraphs or to the condensation, and this restriction is a modeling choice that must be reported, not a technicality.

4.2 Phase 2: pre-registered model comparison on agent benchmarks

Fit four rival models to milestone-level regression outcomes:

· M_0: constant per-step error, no dependency structure.
· M_1: self-conditioning, errors increase subsequent error probability, no graph.
· M_2: regression percolation, the model of Section 2.
· M_3: world-model collapse, internal model degradation independent of graph.

Use repository as a random effect. Compare out-of-sample log-likelihood on held-out repositories. To allow the structural claim to fail, add three structural nulls:

· M_2^{\mathrm{rewire}}: degree-preserving rewiring that destroys the in/out correlation \langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}k^{w}_{\mathrm{out}}\rangle while preserving the in- and out-degree sequences.
· M_2^{\mathrm{rewire\text{-}mod}}: modularity-preserving rewiring, in which edge swaps are constrained to remain within modules, preserving degree sequence and module assignment. This null isolates the effect of in/out correlation from the effect of modular structure, and is a methodological contribution of this paper rather than an established standard in the software literature.
· M_2^{\mathrm{hom}}: the percolation model with a single homogenized \rho instead of edge-specific \rho_{uv}.

If M_2 does not outperform all three nulls on held-out repositories, the structural claim is not supported. Pre-register TOST equivalence margins for the claim that stronger models do not remove the saturation level; a non-significant interaction coefficient is not evidence of invariance without an equivalence margin.

Power analysis is a precondition. Ninety-eight milestones are insufficient to estimate the bounded susceptibility peak of Section 3.3 with standard error below 0.1 on the interaction coefficient. Phase 1 must expand to tens of repositories before Phase 2 kill tests can discriminate between percolation and its rivals.

4.3 Pre-registered interventions

Three orthogonal interventions test the elasticity asymmetry of Theorem 1.

1. p-only: inject synthetic noise into clean patches, raising p without altering q, \rho, \bar b, or \delta. The separability prediction is that \mathbb E[c] responds but \hat R_t does not.
2. q-only: provide dependency-aware test context. TDAD's reduction of regressions from 6.08% to 1.82% is evidence that q_{\mathrm{struct}} is movable by contextual information. The prediction is that \hat R_t falls and the saturation level falls with it. The prediction is not that q_{\mathrm{sem}}\to 1.
3. \bar b-only: apply a dependency pruner or modularizer. The prediction is that \bar b and \hat R_t fall, and that the saturation level falls.

Each intervention is run on held-out repositories with pre-registered effect sizes. The joint signature—p moves level but not \hat R_t, q and \bar b move both—is the empirical content of separability.

4.4 Kill tests

1. Upstream exposure. A milestone's regression probability depends on in-degree and parents' states, not its own out-degree. Sharper: the expected cascade size from a milestone scales with its weighted out-degree and the weighted out-degrees of its descendants, as the Katz formula predicts. Falsification: the cascade-size scaling is flat in descendant weighted out-degree after controlling for size and model.
2. Bounded susceptibility peak. \partial\mathbb E[c]/\partial p is maximized near \hat R_t\approx 1 with height \bar b_{\mathrm{new}}(L_t+1). Falsification: monotone response with no interior maximum, or a maximum whose height does not scale with L_t.
3. Structural null defeat. M_2 outperforms M_2^{\mathrm{rewire}}, M_2^{\mathrm{rewire\text{-}mod}}, and M_2^{\mathrm{hom}} on held-out repositories. Falsification: the modularity-preserving null matches M_2, in which case the effect is modularity, not the weighted in/out correlation.
4. Structural × semantic interaction. Cross structural coverage (high/low) with semantic oracle strength (property-based/example-based). The percolation model predicts that structural coverage alone does not remove the saturation level. Falsification: semantic oracle strength has no effect on the collapse density.

