5. Relation to Prior Work, With Attribution

Change propagation and technical debt. MacCormack, Rusnak, and Baldwin define propagation cost through the visibility matrix and show that modularity-oriented redesign can reduce it by an order of magnitude. Clarkson, Simons, and Eckert model change propagation with heterogeneous edge weights. Geipel and Schweitzer show that propagation is sparse and concentrated, and caution that the dependency structure alone is a poor measure of change dynamics. Regression Percolation inherits this tradition and adds a branching criterion rather than a cost criterion: it asks not only how much propagation occurs but whether the expected cascade exceeds the repair capacity. The technical-debt literature supplies the measurement vocabulary for \delta and for regression-test debt.

Regression testing and impact analysis. Test impact analysis selects tests most likely to be affected by a diff using dependency graphs; impact analysis via transitive closure detects a substantial fraction of potentially breaking changes. These are the practical counterpart of q(n): they raise detection probability at fixed budget for structurally reachable defects. The model predicts that impact analysis raises q_{\mathrm{struct}} but cannot raise q_{\mathrm{sem}} for defects whose oracle is weak.

Capacity monism. The claim that growing capacity decouples solution capability from human cognitive limits is correct that capacity lowers p. It is incomplete because it does not model q, \rho, \bar b, or \delta. The separability theorem makes the gap precise: capacity crosses the structural threshold only if it acts on those parameters.

Brooks. Essential complexity belongs to the problem, not the solution. The branching parameter R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}} is a structural analogue, essential only relative to a fixed verifier and repair technology. If q or \delta improves, the threshold moves. This is a demotion of the Brooks analogy to a conditional structural statement.

Directed percolation. Schwartz, Cohen, Ben-Avraham, Barabási, and Havlin derived the phase diagram for directed scale-free networks with in/out correlations, and showed that in one regime the network is resilient to random dilution with p_c\to 1. That result is a theoretical motivation for using a correlation-dependent critical parameter. It is not a quantitative prediction for software dependency graphs, which are neither scale-free random graphs nor generated by random dilution. The non-backtracking operator is a standard correction for clustering on locally tree-like cyclic graphs; on a DAG it is nilpotent and cannot serve as a criticality criterion.

Lehman. The laws of software evolution characterize E-type processes as multi-level, multi-loop, multi-agent feedback systems. That characterization motivates closing the loop in Section 3.4. It does not supply a mathematical form and makes no criticality claim. The self-organized-criticality prediction is ours and is falsifiable.

---

6. Limitations

1. Branching idealization. The Katz cascade assumes independent propagation channels. On locally tree-like cyclic graphs the non-backtracking operator corrects for backtracking; on a DAG it is nilpotent, and on modular software graphs neither operator fully accounts for joint-degree correlations. The condensation step partially addresses cycles but does not eliminate the need for a joint weighted-degree description.
2. Bounded depth. Proposition 1 shows that the asymptotic criticality object is vacuous for bounded depth. The frontier branching ratio is a finite-sequence substitute, and its sampling theory is only sketched here. Finite-sample bias, confidence intervals, and power at the observed scale remain to be developed.
3. Verifier extrapolation. Mutation scores are not agent regressions. The direct detection curve may be infeasible at 98 milestones. Until it is measured, the heavy-tail hypothesis is a conjecture.
4. Repair rate. \delta is under-measured for agent-generated regressions. Human repair-time distributions provide a lower bound at best.
5. Endogenous graph. The drift model assumes the agent observes \mathbb E[c] and can choose d_v. Current agents operate under partial observability. The drift prediction is testable only under controlled information conditions.
6. Power. The repository count is small for interaction effects and susceptibility peaks. Phase 1 expansion is a precondition, not a refinement.
7. Novel nulls. The modularity-preserving rewiring null is adapted from network neuroscience and has no established software-engineering precedent. Its adoption is a methodological proposal.

---

7. Conclusion

Regression Percolation is a conditional, separability-based theory of structural sustainability. Its central object is the frontier branching ratio on a growing weighted dependency DAG, with the expected cascade size as the observable and the Katz resolvent as the exact finite-depth computation. Its central claim is that if capacity acts only on injection probability, capacity cannot cross the structural threshold; the empirical question is the elasticity of the threshold with respect to q, \rho, \bar b, and \delta. The theory predicts saturation of the infected fraction without an undirected endemic equilibrium, a bounded susceptibility peak near the branching threshold, and a normative drift toward lower out-degree under regression pressure. The infinite-family Katz growth rate is proven vacuous for bounded depth, and the frontier branching ratio replaces it as a measurable object on finite milestone sequences. The empirical program is two-phase and pre-registered, with degree-preserving, modularity-preserving, and homogenized-\rho nulls that allow the structural claim to fail. The structural question is empirically decidable, and the theory is designed to be falsified by the data it motivates.


Appendices


Appendix A: Bounded-Depth Degeneracy of the Katz Growth Rate

A.1 Setting. Let G_n=(V_n,E_n) be a sequence of directed acyclic graphs with |V_n|=n and adjacency matrix A_n. Let L_n be the length of the longest directed path in G_n and d_n the maximum out-degree. The Katz resolvent is

c_n(\tilde\beta)=\bigl(I-\tilde\beta A_n\bigr)^{-1}\mathbf{1}
=\sum_{k=0}^{L_n}\tilde\beta^k A_n^k\mathbf{1},

a finite sum because A_n^{L_n+1}=0. The Katz growth rate is

R_{\mathrm{Katz}}(\tilde\beta)=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\log\|c_n(\tilde\beta)\|_1 .

Proposition A.1 (bounded-depth degeneracy). If L_n=o(n) and d_n=O(1), then R_{\mathrm{Katz}}(\tilde\beta)=0 for every \tilde\beta>0.

Proof. For each k, every component of A_n^k\mathbf{1} is at most d_n^k, because there are at most d_n^k paths of length k starting from any vertex, and each path contributes at most one endpoint. Hence

\|c_n(\tilde\beta)\|_\infty
\le\sum_{k=0}^{L_n}(\tilde\beta d_n)^k .

If \tilde\beta d_n\le 1, then \|c_n\|_\infty\le L_n+1. If \tilde\beta d_n>1, the geometric series is bounded by a constant multiple of (\tilde\beta d_n)^{L_n}. In either case there is a constant C_{\tilde\beta,d} such that

\|c_n(\tilde\beta)\|_\infty
\le C_{\tilde\beta,d}\,(L_n+1)\max\{1,(\tilde\beta d_n)^{L_n}\}.

Therefore

\frac{1}{n}\log\|c_n\|_1
\le\frac{\log C_{\tilde\beta,d}}{n}
+\frac{\log(L_n+1)}{n}
+\frac{L_n}{n}\log\max\{1,\tilde\beta d_n\}.

Since L_n=o(n) and d_n=O(1), the right-hand side tends to zero as n\to\infty. The limit therefore exists and equals zero. ∎

Corollary A.2. If \{G_n\} is a graph family with bounded depth L_n\le L, then R_{\mathrm{Katz}}(\tilde\beta)=0 for all \tilde\beta. The Katz growth rate is therefore not a phase-transition object on bounded-depth graph families.

Remark A.3 (why this does not invalidate the Katz resolvent). The resolvent c_n(\tilde\beta) itself is well defined and is the exact expected cascade size at depth. Proposition A.1 rejects only the infinite-family growth rate as an operationalization of criticality. The replacement on finite sequences is the frontier branching ratio \hat R_t of Section 2.4, not R_{\mathrm{Katz}}.

