Appendix B: Separability Theorem and Elasticities

B.1 Formal proof in the mean-field case.

Theorem B.1 (separability). Suppose model capacity C acts on the injection probability p but not on q, \rho, \bar b, or \delta. Then in the mean-field parameterization

R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}
=\gamma(1-q)\,\frac{\langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}k^{w}_{\mathrm{out}}\rangle}
{\langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}\rangle},
\qquad \gamma=1-\delta,

R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}} is independent of p. Consequently, if R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}>1 and depth is unbounded, the expected cascade size is unbounded for every p>0, and no increase in C that does not change q, \rho, \bar b, or \delta changes the regime.

Proof. The injection probability enters the process only through the immigration rate \iota=p'\bar b=p(1-q)\bar b. The branching parameter R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}} is defined by the mean of the offspring distribution per active site. In a mean-field branching process with independent edges, the offspring mean depends only on the survival probability \gamma, the detection probability q, the propagation probability \rho, and the out-degree moment; it does not depend on the immigration rate. Hence \partial R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}/\partial p=0. Under this condition, criticality R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}>1 holds for every p, and the expected cascade size grows as a power of R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}} for any p>0. ∎

B.2 Elasticities. Define

\varepsilon_x=\frac{x}{R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}}\frac{\partial R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}}{\partial x},
\qquad x\in\{p,q,\rho,\bar b,\delta\}.

In the mean-field form:

· \varepsilon_p=0 (by B.1).
· \varepsilon_q=\dfrac{q}{R}\dfrac{\partial R}{\partial q}=-\dfrac{q}{1-q}.
· \varepsilon_\rho=1, because R is linear in \rho.
· \varepsilon_\delta=\dfrac{\delta}{R}\dfrac{\partial R}{\partial\delta}=-\dfrac{\delta}{1-\delta}, because \gamma=1-\delta.
· \varepsilon_{\bar b} is determined by the logarithmic derivative of the weighted moment \langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}k^{w}_{\mathrm{out}}\rangle/\langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}\rangle with respect to \bar b; it is nonzero unless the moment is invariant to density.

The separability hypothesis predicts

|\varepsilon_q|,\;|\varepsilon_\rho|,\;|\varepsilon_{\bar b}|,\;|\varepsilon_\delta|
\;\gg\;|\varepsilon_p|=0 .

This is the empirical content of the theory. It is an asymmetry to be tested, not a theorem. The mean-field values \varepsilon_q=-q/(1-q) and \varepsilon_\delta=-\delta/(1-\delta) diverge as q,\delta\to 1, reflecting the sensitivity of the branching parameter to detection and repair at high detection or repair rates.

B.3 When elasticities are meaningful at finite depth. Elasticities are local derivatives of the branching parameter. At finite depth the branching parameter is itself approximate (the frontier ratio \hat R_t), so elasticities should be estimated with respect to R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}} rather than \hat R_t, and should be interpreted as sensitivity indices rather than exact critical exponents when L_t is a small constant. Their values near \hat R_t=1 nevertheless govern the magnitude of the response of the level to each structural parameter, which is what the Phase A interventions measure.

---

Appendix C: Saturation, Susceptibility Peak, and Finite-Depth Corrections

C.1 Expected active fraction. In quasi-steady state with immigration rate \iota_t, on a graph G_t of depth L_t, the expected active fraction is approximated by

\frac{\mathbb E|X_t|}{|V_t|}
\approx
\iota_t\sum_{k=0}^{L_t}\hat R_t^{k}
=
\iota_t\frac{1-\hat R_t^{L_t+1}}{1-\hat R_t}
\quad(\hat R_t\neq 1),

and by \iota_t(L_t+1) when \hat R_t=1.

Derivation. Immigration injects \iota_t|V_t| active sites at each step. An active site produces an expected \hat R_t^k active descendants at k steps. Summing over steps up to L_t gives the geometric series. Because L_t is finite, the sum is bounded even when \hat R_t>1. ∎

C.2 Saturation without endemicity. Equation (C.1) is bounded in the supercritical regime because L_t is finite. The active fraction therefore saturates without diverging, and no nontrivial endemic density exists. The relevant contrast with the undirected contact process is that the undirected process has a feedback loop; a DAG does not. The observed EvoClaw regression rates are consistent with this bounded saturation; they are not evidence of an endemic equilibrium.

C.3 Susceptibility peak. Define \chi_p=\partial\mathbb E[c]/\partial p. At depth L_t and mean new out-degree \bar b_{\mathrm{new}},

\chi_p
=
\bar b_{\mathrm{new}}\,
\frac{1-\hat R_t^{L_t+1}}{1-\hat R_t}
\qquad(\hat R_t\neq 1),

which is maximized at \hat R_t=1 with value \bar b_{\mathrm{new}}(L_t+1) and decays to \bar b_{\mathrm{new}} as \hat R_t\to 0.

Proof. The immigration rate \iota_t=p'\bar b_{\mathrm{new}} is linear in p, so \partial\iota_t/\partial p=\bar b_{\mathrm{new}}. Differentiating (C.1) with respect to p while holding \hat R_t fixed (justified by separability, \partial\hat R_t/\partial p\approx 0) gives \chi_p=\bar b_{\mathrm{new}}\sum_{k=0}^{L_t}\hat R_t^k, which is the stated geometric series. Its value at \hat R_t=1 is evidently \bar b_{\mathrm{new}}(L_t+1), and it tends to \bar b_{\mathrm{new}} as \hat R_t\to 0. ∎

The prediction is therefore a peak of finite height, not the divergence obtained in infinite directed percolation. The peak height grows linearly in L_t. This is the falsifiable prediction in Section 4.4, item 2.

C.4 Finite-depth corrections. When the depth L_t is finite, the branching parameter \hat R_t and its elasticities are approximations. The level is bounded by (C.1) for R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}>1, and decays as \hat R_t^{L_t} for R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}<1. The relevant scale for correction across repository comparisons is L_t, not n. This is the quantity to report in the expansion described in the main text.

