Appendix D: Estimator Properties

D.1 Frontier branching ratio \hat R_t. The estimator is

\hat R_t
=
\gamma\,
\frac{1}{|\mathcal F_t|}
\sum_{v\in\mathcal F_t}\ \sum_{u:\,v\to u}\rho_{vu},

where \mathcal F_t is the active frontier and \rho_{vu} is fitted in Phase A. Under the assumption of independent observations of edge weights and bounded \rho_{vu}, \hat R_t is consistent as |\mathcal F_t|\to\infty with bias O(|\mathcal F_t|^{-1/2}). The variance is bounded by the sample variance of the observed \rho_{vu} times 1/|\mathcal F_t|. When \mathcal F_t is small—early milestones, or sparse active sets after high detection—the interval is wide, and \hat R_t should be reported as an interval estimate rather than a point estimate.

D.2 Weighted degree moments. The estimator

\widehat{R}^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}}
=
\gamma(1-q)\,
\frac{\widehat{\langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}k^{w}_{\mathrm{out}}\rangle}}
{\widehat{\langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}\rangle}}

uses empirical joint weighted degree moments from Phase A. The estimated ratio is sensitive to noise in the denominator estimate; intervals should be obtained by the delta method. If the estimated \langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}\rangle is small—which occurs when a large fraction of dependencies have zero propagation probability—the interval is wide and R^{w}_{\mathrm{DP}} may be unidentifiable. This is precisely why the Geipel–Schweitzer sparsity result matters: zero inflation drives the estimated mean toward zero and destabilizes the moment ratio. The practical correction is stratification by dependency type, with per-stratum and pooled estimates reported.

D.3 Detection curve q(n). The curve q(n)=\mathbb E[1-(1-\mu)^n] must be estimated directly on agent-produced regressions. For a fixed budget n, the empirical kill fraction on the regression set is the plug-in estimate of q(n). The shape of the curve across n distinguishes heavy tails from bounded support. If the kill fraction at moderate n remains well below 1 and improves sub-logarithmically in n, the heavy-tail hypothesis is supported. The feasibility of estimating this on 98 EvoClaw milestones is an open empirical question (Appendix H).

---

Appendix E: Structural Null Models

E.1 M_2^{\mathrm{rewire}}: degree-preserving rewiring. Edges are swapped so that the in-degree and out-degree sequences are preserved exactly and no self-loops are created. This null destroys the in/out correlation \langle k^{w}_{\mathrm{in}}k^{w}_{\mathrm{out}}\rangle while preserving the degree distributions. If the full percolation model does not outperform this null out of sample, the structural claim of correlation does not survive.

E.2 M_2^{\mathrm{rewire\text{-}mod}}: modularity-preserving rewiring. Swaps are constrained so that new edges lie in the same module as the old ones. The procedure preserves degree sequences and module assignment. It adapts the constrained edge-swap procedure from network neuroscience, in which swaps are accepted only when module labels agree—for example, the constraint that (u,x) and (w,v) must also be in H_c^{(l)}, together with degree-preserving swaps that conserve within-module and between-module edge counts. No established software-engineering precedent for this null exists; its adoption is a methodological proposal of this paper. Its function is to isolate the effect of the in/out correlation from the effect of modular structure. If M_2 outperforms M_2^{\mathrm{rewire}} but not M_2^{\mathrm{rewire\text{-}mod}}, the effect is due to modularity, not to the correlation moment.

E.3 M_2^{\mathrm{hom}}: homogenized \rho. Every edge weight is set to its empirical mean. This null tests whether heterogeneity matters. The Geipel–Schweitzer sparsity and concentration results make the null mandatory: if more than half of the edges have zero weight and a minority of edges carries the majority of propagation, homogenizing \rho is a mis-specification, not a simplification.

E.4 Hierarchy of nulls. The nulls are ordered by retained structure: M_2^{\mathrm{hom}} (mean only), M_2^{\mathrm{rewire}} (degree sequence, no correlation), M_2^{\mathrm{rewire\text{-}mod}} (degree sequence plus modularity), and the full M_2 (weighted correlation). Out-of-sample performance must increase monotonically across each retained structure; otherwise the corresponding structural claim is not supported.

---

Appendix F: Pre-Registered Interventions

F.1 p-only intervention. Inject synthetic noise into clean patches, raising p without changing q, \rho, \bar b, or \delta. By separability, \mathbb E[c] should rise but \hat R_t should not. An observed shift in \hat R_t would falsify separability.

F.2 q-only intervention. Provide dependency-aware test context. TDAD's evidence that regressions fall from 6.08% to 1.82% shows that q_{\mathrm{struct}} is movable by contextual information. Both \hat R_t and the saturation level should fall. The intervention does not predict q_{\mathrm{sem}}\to 1; it moves the structural channel only.

F.3 \bar b-only intervention. Apply a dependency pruner or modularizer. Both \bar b and \hat R_t should fall, and the saturation level should fall with them.

F.4 Joint signature. The empirical content of separability is the pattern: p moves the level but not \hat R_t; q and \bar b move both the level and \hat R_t. The joint signature should be tested as a pre-registered hypothesis on held-out repositories, with effect-size bounds.

